@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Dropping Tables";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->
    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Dropping Tables
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            In SQL Server, the DROP TABLE statement is used to permanently
            remove an existing table from a database. In this section,
            we will learn how to safely drop tables and understand what
            happens when a table is deleted.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT IS DROP TABLE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is DROP TABLE?
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>DROP TABLE</strong> statement completely removes
            a table from the database.
        </p>

        <p>
            When a table is dropped, its structure and stored records
            are removed.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>DROP TABLE Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DROP TABLE FLOW -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DROP TABLE Flow
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL Server
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>Students Table
       ↓
DROP TABLE
       ↓
Table Removed
       ↓
Data + Table Structure Deleted</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SIMPLE EXAMPLE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Drop a Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we have a table named <strong>Students</strong>.
            The following statement removes it:
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>DROP TABLE Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            After executing this statement, the Students table will no
            longer exist in the database.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- BEFORE DROP -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Table Before DROP
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>Course</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>1</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Java</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>2</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                        <td>.NET</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>3</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Python</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DROP COMMAND -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Execute DROP TABLE
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>DROP TABLE Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- VERIFY TABLE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Verify Whether the Table Exists
        </h2>

        <p>
            Before performing operations, we can check whether a table
            exists in the current database.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES
WHERE TABLE_NAME = 'Students';</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            If the table has been dropped successfully, the query will
            not return the Students table.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DROP IF EXISTS -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DROP TABLE IF EXISTS
        </h2>

        <p>
            SQL Server also supports <strong>DROP TABLE IF EXISTS</strong>.
            It is useful when we want to drop a table only if it exists.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            This avoids an error when the specified table does not exist.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DROP MULTIPLE TABLES -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Dropping Multiple Tables
        </h2>

        <p>
            Multiple tables can be dropped by using separate DROP TABLE
            statements.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Students;

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Courses;

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS Departments;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DROP VS DELETE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DROP vs DELETE
        </h2>

        <p>
            DROP and DELETE are different SQL operations.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>DROP TABLE</th>
                        <th>DELETE</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Removes the complete table.</td>
                        <td>Removes rows from a table.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Table structure is removed.</td>
                        <td>Table structure remains.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Table no longer exists.</td>
                        <td>Table still exists.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Used to remove a database table.</td>
                        <td>Used to remove records.</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DROP VS TRUNCATE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DROP vs TRUNCATE
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Operation</th>
                        <th>Table Structure</th>
                        <th>Data</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>DROP</strong></td>
                        <td>Removed</td>
                        <td>Removed</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>TRUNCATE</strong></td>
                        <td>Remains</td>
                        <td>Removed</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT WARNING -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-danger border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                ⚠ Important Warning
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">
                DROP TABLE permanently removes the table structure and
                its data. Always verify the table name before executing
                a DROP TABLE statement, especially when working with
                important or production databases.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- PRACTICAL EXAMPLE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Practical Example
        </h2>

        <p>
            The following example creates a temporary table and then
            removes it.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE DemoStudents
(
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100)
);

DROP TABLE DemoStudents;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- RESULT -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Result
        </h2>

        <div class="alert alert-secondary border-0">

            <p class="mb-0">
                After executing DROP TABLE, the DemoStudents table is
                removed from the database along with its table structure
                and stored records.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->
    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned how to remove a table using
                DROP TABLE, how to check whether a table exists, and the
                difference between DROP, DELETE and TRUNCATE operations.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


